    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds an optional `email` field to pet owners on `POST /api/owners`: when present it must be a syntactically valid address, and it's stored and returned lower-cased; an invalid address yields **400**.\n\n**Files changed:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added an `email` property (`type: string`, `format: email`) to the `OwnerFields` schema. This flows into the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto`, so email is accepted on input and serialized on output. Because `performBeanValidation` is on and the request body is `@Valid`, the generator's `@Email` constraint rejects a malformed address with 400 (confirmed in the test log).\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `email` field (`@Column(name = \"email\")`, `@Email`) with getter/setter and a `toString` entry.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 In `addOwner`, after mapping, if `email` is present it's trimmed, validated against a syntactic email pattern (returning 400 on failure), and lower-cased before saving. This guarantees both the reject-and-lowercase behavior independently of the generated constraint.\n\n4. **DB schema** (`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`) \u2014 Added a nullable `email` column to the `owners` table. Tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in the script.\n\n5. **DB seed data** (`db/{hsqldb,mysql}/data.sql`) \u2014 The seed owners used positional `INSERT INTO owners VALUES (...)`, which broke once a column was added. I gave them explicit column lists so the new `email` defaults to NULL. (h2/postgres already used explicit lists.)\n\n**Tests:** `AcceptanceTest` (the only test class) passes 3/3 \u2014 `coreLowercasesEmail`, `errorRejectsInvalidEmail`, `functionalityAcceptsMissingEmail` \u2014 and the full `mvn test` run reports **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
